% 49-self-hosting-vec.weft — Stage 8c: the growable vector, written in C,
% compiled by kcc itself, and linked back into the compiler.

\chapter{Self-Hosting: The Vector in C}
\label{ch:self-hosting-vec}

The arena (Chapter~\ref{ch:self-hosting-arena}) proved \kcc{} can compile a
piece of its own \emph{storage} layer; this chapter climbs one level up and
ports a \emph{data structure}---the growable vector of
Chapter~\ref{ch:data-structures}.  The vector is the workhorse container of the
whole compiler: the list of IR functions, each function's basic blocks, the
symbol-table scopes, the relocation and symbol vectors of the linker, and the
back end's patch lists are all \texttt{kcc\_vec}s.  Re-implementing it in C,
compiling that C with \kcc{}, and linking the result back in place of the
hand-written assembly is therefore a second, independent self-hosting step:
where the arena stressed one allocation path, the vector stresses element
copying, the geometric-growth reallocation, and pointer arithmetic on a
caller-supplied element size---exercised millions of times across the suite.

\section{Interface and Layout}

The C port must be a drop-in replacement, so it exports exactly the three
symbols the assembly vector did---\texttt{kcc\_vec\_init},
\texttt{kcc\_vec\_push}, and \texttt{kcc\_vec\_get}---and it reads and writes a
header whose fields sit at the very offsets the rest of the compiler already
hard-codes: \texttt{data} at~0, \texttt{count} at~8, \texttt{cap} at~16,
\texttt{elem\_size} at~24.  Because \kcc{}'s all-spill layout gives every scalar
struct field its own eight-byte slot, the natural C declaration---a pointer
followed by three \texttt{unsigned long}s---occupies exactly those offsets, so
the C header and the assembly header are the same 32 bytes byte for byte.  The
port depends only on the two primitives the assembly version used, the arena
allocator and \texttt{memcpy}, declared here as ordinary \texttt{extern}
functions the linker resolves across objects.

@l c
@d Selfhost: vec preamble @{
struct Vec {
    void *data;
    unsigned long count;
    unsigned long cap;
    unsigned long elem_size;
};

void *kcc_arena_alloc(long size);
void  kcc_memcpy(void *dst, const void *src, unsigned long n);
@}

\section{Initialization}

\texttt{kcc\_vec\_init} arena-allocates the 32-byte header and an initial
backing array for sixteen elements, mirroring the assembly routine exactly: the
count starts at zero and the caller never specifies a capacity.

@l c
@d Selfhost: vec init @{
void *kcc_vec_init(unsigned long elem_size) {
    struct Vec *v = (struct Vec *)kcc_arena_alloc(sizeof(struct Vec));
    v->data = kcc_arena_alloc((long)(16 * elem_size));
    v->count = 0;
    v->cap = 16;
    v->elem_size = elem_size;
    return v;
}
@}

\section{Push}

\texttt{kcc\_vec\_push} appends a copy of the caller's element.  When the vector
is full it doubles the capacity, allocates a fresh array, and copies the live
elements across before storing the new one---the geometric growth that keeps a
sequence of $n$ pushes amortised linear.  Both copies go through the same
\texttt{memcpy} the assembly used, and all sizes are computed from the header's
\texttt{elem\_size}, so a vector of any element width behaves identically.

@l c
@d Selfhost: vec push @{
void kcc_vec_push(void *vec, const void *elem) {
    struct Vec *v = (struct Vec *)vec;
    if (v->count >= v->cap) {
        unsigned long newcap = v->cap * 2;
        void *nd = kcc_arena_alloc((long)(newcap * v->elem_size));
        kcc_memcpy(nd, v->data, v->count * v->elem_size);
        v->data = nd;
        v->cap = newcap;
    }
    kcc_memcpy((char *)v->data + v->count * v->elem_size, elem, v->elem_size);
    v->count = v->count + 1;
}
@}

\section{Element Access}

\texttt{kcc\_vec\_get} returns a pointer to the element at an index, computed as
\texttt{data + index * elem\_size}, with no bounds checking---the caller
guarantees the index is in range, exactly as the assembly contract required.

@l c
@d Selfhost: vec get @{
void *kcc_vec_get(void *vec, unsigned long index) {
    struct Vec *v = (struct Vec *)vec;
    return (char *)v->data + index * v->elem_size;
}
@}

\section{Assembling the Translation Unit}

The three fragments compose into one C file, \texttt{tmp/selfhost/vec.c}, routed
by \texttt{assembly.weft}.

@l c
@d Selfhost: vec @{
@<Selfhost: vec preamble@>
@<Selfhost: vec init@>
@<Selfhost: vec push@>
@<Selfhost: vec get@>
@}

\section{Verification}
\label{sec:selfhost-vec-verify}

\texttt{make selfhost-vec} runs the same drop-in loop the arena uses.  It
compiles \texttt{tmp/selfhost/vec.c} with the assembly-built \kcc{}, relinks a
second compiler binary with the hand-written \texttt{vec.o} swapped for the
freshly compiled \texttt{vec\_c.o}, and points the code-generation suite at it.

The passing criterion is again complete: the self-hosted compiler must
reproduce every result of the assembly-built one.  Because vectors carry the IR
function list, every function's basic blocks, and the symbol-table scopes, a
green run means the C vector---compiled by \kcc{}---is behaviourally
byte-for-byte equivalent to the assembly original, growth path and all.

@d Selfhost: vec verify @{
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# make selfhost-vec — compile kcc's vector in C with kcc, relink, run the suite.
set -euo pipefail

SDK=$(xcrun --show-sdk-path)
SH=tmp/selfhost
sources="main syscalls arena strings io hashtab vec strtab source lexer \
lexer_tables diag preprocessor parser expressions statements declarations \
types symtab conversions sema ir opt emit_arm64 emit_x86 linker codegen emit_macho dwarf driver"

echo "[1/3] compiling tmp/selfhost/vec.c with kcc"
tmp/kcc -c "$SH/vec.c" -o "$SH/vec_c.o" >/dev/null

echo "[2/3] linking tmp/kcc_selfhost_vec (vec.o -> kcc-compiled vec_c.o)"
OBJ=""
for f in $sources; do
    if [ "$f" = "vec" ]; then OBJ="$OBJ $SH/vec_c.o"; else OBJ="$OBJ tmp/src/${f}.o"; fi
done
ld -o tmp/kcc_selfhost_vec $OBJ -lSystem -syslibroot "$SDK" -e _main -arch arm64

echo "[3/3] running the codegen suite with the self-hosted vector"
cp tmp/kcc "$SH/kcc_asm_backup_vec"
cp tmp/kcc_selfhost_vec tmp/kcc
set +e
bash tmp/tests/test_codegen.sh
status=$?
cp "$SH/kcc_asm_backup_vec" tmp/kcc
exit $status
@}
